{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-104321" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nf(y,z)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,y,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(y,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $5x=10{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}.$ What is the first component of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-104321" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Solving our equation for $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n5x=10{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=2{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}{z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}{z}^{2},\\,y,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $2{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}{z}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-104321" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c82fe7814da75f780ed20aabef787d42b88088ae491cb3047a018b2fc098949"} {"question" "q-104321" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-104321" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}{z}^{2}"] ["math" "9{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}{z}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{9{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}}"] ["math" "y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-104321" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}{z}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nf(x,z)=⟨x,\\,\\text{_____},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x,z)$ above parametrizes the hyperbolic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $3y={z}^{2}-9{x}^{2}.$ What is the second component of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Solving our equation for $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n3y={z}^{2}-9{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}-3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}-3{x}^{2},\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}-3{x}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c82fe7814da75f780ed20aabef787d42b88088ae491cb3047a018b2fc098949"} {"question" "q-164465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}+{x}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}-3{x}^{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-3{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}}"] ["math" "{z}^{2}-3{x}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}-3{x}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nf(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x,y)$ above parametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{z}{2}=3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}.$ What is the third component of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Solving our equation for $z,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{z}{2}=3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\,⟹\\,z=6{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,6{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $6{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c82fe7814da75f780ed20aabef787d42b88088ae491cb3047a018b2fc098949"} {"question" "q-164463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}"] ["math" "6{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164469" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nf(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x,y)$ above parametrizes the hyperbolic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{2z}{3}=4{x}^{2}-6{y}^{2}.$ What is the third component of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164469" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Solving our equation for $z,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{2z}{3}=4{x}^{2}-6{y}^{2}\\,⟹\\,z=6{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,6{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$   \n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $6{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164469" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c82fe7814da75f780ed20aabef787d42b88088ae491cb3047a018b2fc098949"} {"question" "q-164469" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164469" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}"] ["math" "2{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}"] ["math" "4{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}"] ["math" "6{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}"] ["math" "6{y}^{2}-9{x}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164469" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nf(y,z)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,y,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(y,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $4x=16{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}.$ What is the first component of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Solving our equation for $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n4x=16{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=4{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{2},\\,y,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $4{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c82fe7814da75f780ed20aabef787d42b88088ae491cb3047a018b2fc098949"} {"question" "q-164464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}"] ["math" "4{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}}"] ["math" "{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164538" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨\\text{_____},\\,{r}^{2},\\,5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3Y,\\,y=Z,\\,z=5X\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $f.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164538" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3Y,\\,y=Z,\\,z=5X\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3Y,Z,5X⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z,\\,5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2},\\,5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$  \n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164538" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c82fe7814da75f780ed20aabef787d42b88088ae491cb3047a018b2fc098949"} {"question" "q-164538" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164538" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\sqrt{5r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164538" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨{r}^{2},\\,2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+{z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=Z,\\,y=2X,\\,z=Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=Z,\\,y=2X,\\,z=Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get \n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,2X,Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,\\,2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{r}^{2},\\,2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c82fe7814da75f780ed20aabef787d42b88088ae491cb3047a018b2fc098949"} {"question" "q-164541" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164541" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{r}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\frac{r}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164541" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164529" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,{r}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=4X,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the second component of $f.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164529" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=4X,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4X,\\sqrt{3}Y,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\sqrt{3}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\sqrt{3}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $\\sqrt{3}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164529" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c82fe7814da75f780ed20aabef787d42b88088ae491cb3047a018b2fc098949"} {"question" "q-164529" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164529" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\sqrt{3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "r"] ["math" "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164529" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{3}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨{r}^{2},\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=Z,\\,y=3X,\\,z=2Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=Z,\\,y=3X,\\,z=2Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,3X,2Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{r}^{2},\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c82fe7814da75f780ed20aabef787d42b88088ae491cb3047a018b2fc098949"} {"question" "q-164539" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164539" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\sqrt{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }"] ["math" "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta "] ["math" "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164539" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2},\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3Y,\\,y=Z,\\,z=2X\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3Y,\\,y=Z,\\,z=2X\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3Y,Z,2X⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z,\\,2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2},\\,2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c82fe7814da75f780ed20aabef787d42b88088ae491cb3047a018b2fc098949"} {"question" "q-164536" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164536" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2r"] ["math" "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta "] ["math" "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164536" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=X,\\,y=6Z,\\,z=\\sqrt{2}Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r,Z⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=X,\\,y=6Z,\\,z=\\sqrt{2}Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨r,Z⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨X,6Z,\\sqrt{2}Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,6Z,\\,\\sqrt{2}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c82fe7814da75f780ed20aabef787d42b88088ae491cb3047a018b2fc098949"} {"question" "q-164585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-104007" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=16.$ By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the second component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{z}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r,z⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-104007" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{z}^{2}=16$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $x$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨r,z⟩=⟨4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-104007" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c82fe7814da75f780ed20aabef787d42b88088ae491cb3047a018b2fc098949"} {"question" "q-104007" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-104007" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/refe