\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨r,Z⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨X,4Y,2Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164587" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c82fe7814da75f780ed20aabef787d42b88088ae491cb3047a018b2fc098949"} {"question" "q-164587" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164587" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164587" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164571" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_______}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=25.$ By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{z}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r,z⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164571" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $(r,y,\\theta )$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =25 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{z}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{z}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{z}^{2}=25$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $x$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨r,z⟩=⟨5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164571" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c82fe7814da75f780ed20aabef787d42b88088ae491cb3047a018b2fc098949"} {"question" "q-164571" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164571" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164571" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-104008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-{y}^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3X,\\,y=Z,\\,z=2Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r,Z⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-104008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3X,\\,y=Z,\\,z=2Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨r,Z⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3X,Z,2Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,2(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-104008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c82fe7814da75f780ed20aabef787d42b88088ae491cb3047a018b2fc098949"} {"question" "q-104008" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-104008" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-104008" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=4X,\\,y=3Z,\\,z=\\sqrt{3}\\,Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the second component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${Z}^{2}-{r}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨Z,r⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=4X,\\,y=3Z,\\,z=\\sqrt{3}\\,Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {Z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${Z}^{2}-{r}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨Z,r⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4X,3Z,\\sqrt{3}\\,Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,3Z,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\,(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c82fe7814da75f780ed20aabef787d42b88088ae491cb3047a018b2fc098949"} {"question" "q-164580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3Z,\\,y=4Y,\\,z=2X\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${Z}^{2}-{r}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨Z,r⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3Z,\\,y=4Y,\\,z=2X\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {Z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${Z}^{2}-{r}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨Z,r⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3Z,4Y,2X⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3Z,\\,4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,4(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c82fe7814da75f780ed20aabef787d42b88088ae491cb3047a018b2fc098949"} {"question" "q-164586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=-16.$ By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the second component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${z}^{2}-{r}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨z,r⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{z}^{2} & =-16 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-16 \\\\ {z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${z}^{2}-{r}^{2}=16$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $x$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨z,r⟩=⟨4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c82fe7814da75f780ed20aabef787d42b88088ae491cb3047a018b2fc098949"} {"question" "q-164576" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164576" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164576" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=-25.$ By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${z}^{2}-{r}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨z,r⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{z}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${z}^{2}-{r}^{2}=25$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $x$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨z,r⟩=⟨5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c82fe7814da75f780ed20aabef787d42b88088ae491cb3047a018b2fc098949"} {"question" "q-164570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-{z}^{2}=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=5X,\\,y=2Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${Z}^{2}-{r}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨Z,r⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=5X,\\,y=2Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {Z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${Z}^{2}-{r}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨Z,r⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5X,2Y,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6c82fe7814da75f780ed20aabef787d42b88088ae491cb3047a018b2fc098949"} {"question" "q-164581" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164581" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "q-164581" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "2f01fcf53d4d2994f6b02f57b98f5dfed254bb5b3db70a8dd8ea878590daaef6"} {"question" "e-8891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(y, z) =  \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(y, z)$ above parametrizes the hyperbolic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $-3x=-6y^2+3z^2.$ What is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/example-8891.json" "file_sha256" "e2d5a242155e9c32398c284839939c3444a4974f97092f7c072bc70733ae55f3"} {"question" "e-8891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Solving our equation for $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n-3x=-6y^2+3z^2 \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx=2y^2-z^2.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2},\\,y,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ and $z \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue} 2y^2-z^2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/example-8891.json" "file_sha256" "e2d5a242155e9c32398c284839939c3444a4974f97092f7c072bc70733ae55f3"} {"question" "e-12561" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta),$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) =  \\big\\langle \\text{_____} , \\: r^2, \\: 2 r \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\ny=4{x^2}+\\dfrac{z^2}{4}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx =\\dfrac1{2}X, \\qquad\ny = Z, \\qquad\nz = 2Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $\\mathbf{f}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/example-12561.json" "file_sha256" "4420b4f1f9bc6f58d3ec8a5f8a484f1ea7d03e8c0e93270f18c2ae4f403f2259"} {"question" "e-12561" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx =\\dfrac1{2}X, \\qquad\ny = Z, \\qquad\nz = 2Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get \n\n$$\nZ = X^2 + Y^2.\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\nY = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nZ = z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}X,Z,2Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2},\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r \\in [0,\\infty)$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue} \\dfrac1{2} r \\cos\\theta}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/example-12561.json" "file_sha256" "4420b4f1f9bc6f58d3ec8a5f8a484f1ea7d03e8c0e93270f18c2ae4f403f2259"} {"question" "e-8886" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi),$ given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) =  \\big\\langle 3\\cosh{\\phi} \\cos\\theta, \\: 4\\sinh{\\phi}, \\: \\text{______} \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{9}-\\dfrac{y^2}{16}+z^2=1.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx = 3X, \\qquad\ny = 4Z, \\qquad\nz = Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $\\mathbf{f}.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola $r^2-Z^2 = k^2$ can be parametrized as $\\langle r, Z\\rangle = \\langle k\\cosh\\phi, k\\sinh \\phi\\rangle$* for $\\phi\\in \\mathbb R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/example-8886.json" "file_sha256" "d72d32beee2c5cec1686c55fd4ee5fdbbb6af4dc47e4ec2505766d5ac0bd88d5"} {"question" "e-8886" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx = 3X, \\qquad\ny = 4Z, \\qquad\nz = Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nX^2 + Y^2 - Z^2 = 1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\nY = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nZ = Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola $r^2 - Z^2 = 1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta$ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n\\langle r, Z \\rangle = \\langle \\cosh{\\phi}, \\: \\sinh{\\phi} \\rangle,\n\\qquad \\phi \\in \\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3X,4Z,Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4Z,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $\\phi \\in \\mathbb R.$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue} \\cosh{\\phi} \\sin\\theta }.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/example-8886.json" "file_sha256" "d72d32beee2c5cec1686c55fd4ee5fdbbb6af4dc47e4ec2505766d5ac0bd88d5"} {"question" "e-8890" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi),$ given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) =  \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: 3\\sinh{\\phi} \\sin\\theta, \\: 3\\cosh{\\phi} \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x^2 + y^2 - z^2 = -9.$ By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $\\mathbf{f}.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola $z^2-r^2 = k^2$ can be parametrized as $\\langle z,r\\rangle = \\langle k\\cosh\\phi, k\\sinh \\phi\\rangle$* for $\\phi\\in \\mathbb R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/example-8890.json" "file_sha256" "7cf04bfbc49e80605199adb8521d9a937dfdff68220f66da0a3211f625a371a0"} {"question" "e-8890" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\n\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \n\nz = z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{z}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ {z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola $z^2 - r^2 = 9$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta$ with the positive $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/assets/4e7b508432f12b8dc98e37ae2786d220eb97bdc58fe5a29c5f7728cac46471f0.png)\n\nUsing the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n\\langle z, r \\rangle = \\langle 3\\cosh{\\phi}, \\: 3\\sinh{\\phi} \\rangle,\n\\qquad \\phi \\in \\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $\\phi \\in \\mathbb R.$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}3\\sinh{\\phi} \\cos\\theta} .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/example-8890.json" "file_sha256" "7cf04bfbc49e80605199adb8521d9a937dfdff68220f66da0a3211f625a371a0"}] "usage" {"e-12561" [] "e-8890" [] "e-8886" [] "e-8891" [] "q-104007" [] "q-164541" [] "q-104008" [] "q-104321" []} "basis" 2811 "retractions" [[17592186176124 :question/problem "$f(y, z) = ⟨_____, y, z⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(y, z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $5x = 10y^{2} + 2z^{2}$. What is the first component of $f$?"] [17592186176323 :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(r, θ)$, given by\n\n$f(r, θ) = ⟨r^{2}, 2r\\cos θ, _____⟩$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n$x = \\frac{y^{2}}{4} + z^{2}$.\nBy first considering the transformations\n$x = Z, y = 2X, z = Y$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f$."] [17592186176118 :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(θ, ϕ)$, given by\n\n$f(θ, ϕ) = ⟨4\\cosh ϕ\\cos θ, _____, 4\\sinh ϕ⟩$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x^{2} + y^{2} - z^{2} = 16$. By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the second component of $f$.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola $r^{2} - z^{2} = k^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r, z⟩ = ⟨k\\cosh ϕ, k\\sinh ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ ∈ R$."] [17592186176119 :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(θ, ϕ)$, given by\n\n$f(θ, ϕ) = ⟨_____, \\sinh ϕ, 2\\cosh ϕ\\sin θ⟩$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$\\frac{x^{2}}{9} - y^{2} + \\frac{z^{2}}{4} = 1$.\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n$x = 3X, y = Z, z = 2Y$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $f$.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola $r^{2} - Z^{2} = k^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r, Z⟩ = ⟨k\\cosh ϕ, k\\sinh ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ ∈ R$."] [17592186175747 :question/problem "$\\mathbf{f}(y, z) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(y, z)$ above parametrizes the hyperbolic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $-3x=-6y^2+3z^2$. What is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$?"] [17592186175747 :question/worked-solution "Solving our equation for $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3x=-6y^2+3z^2 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=2y^2-z^2\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(y, z) &= ⟨x, y, z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2y^{2} - z^{2}, y, z⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ and $z \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue} 2y^2-z^2}$."] [17592186175637 :question/problem "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta)$, given by\n\n$\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: r^2, \\: 2 r \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n$y=4{x^2}+\\dfrac{z^2}{4}$.\nBy first considering the transformations\n$x =\\dfrac1{2}X, \\qquad y = Z, \\qquad z = 2Y$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$."] [17592186175637 :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx =\\dfrac1{2}X, \\qquad y = Z, \\qquad z = 2Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nZ = X^2 + Y^2\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad Y = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad Z = z\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nZ &= X^{2} + Y^{2} \\\\\nZ &= (r\\cos θ)^{2} + (r\\sin θ)^{2} \\\\\nZ &= r^{2}(\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ) \\\\\nZ &= r^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r, θ) &= ⟨x, y, z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{1}{2}X, Z, 2Y⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{1}{2}r\\cos θ, Z, 2r\\sin θ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{1}{2}r\\cos θ, r^{2}, 2r\\sin θ⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $[MATH: r \\in [0,\\infty)]$ and $[MATH: \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).]$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue} \\dfrac1{2} r \\cos\\theta}\n$$"] [17592186175743 :question/problem "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi)$, given by\n\n$\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle 3\\cosh{\\phi} \\cos\\theta, \\: 4\\sinh{\\phi}, \\: \\text{______} \\big\\rangle$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$\\dfrac{x^2}{9}-\\dfrac{y^2}{16}+z^2=1$.\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n$x = 3X, \\qquad y = 4Z, \\qquad z = Y$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola $r^2-Z^2 = k^2$ can be parametrized as $\\langle r, Z\\rangle = \\langle k\\cosh\\phi, k\\sinh \\phi\\rangle$* for $\\phi\\in \\mathbb R$."] [17592186175743 :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx = 3X, \\qquad y = 4Z, \\qquad z = Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nX^2 + Y^2 - Z^2 = 1\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad Y = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad Z = Z\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX^{2} + Y^{2} - Z^{2} &= 1 \\\\\n(r\\cos θ)^{2} + (r\\sin θ)^{2} - Z^{2} &= 1 \\\\\nr^{2}(\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ) - Z^{2} &= 1 \\\\\nr^{2} - Z^{2} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola\n\n$$\nr^2 - Z^2 = 1\n$$\n\nrepresents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta$ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n\\langle r, Z \\rangle = \\langle \\cosh{\\phi}, \\: \\sinh{\\phi} \\rangle, \\qquad \\phi \\in \\mathbb R\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(θ, ϕ) &= ⟨x, y, z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3X, 4Z, Y⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3r\\cos θ, 4Z, r\\sin θ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3(\\cosh ϕ)\\cos θ, 4\\sinh ϕ, (\\cosh ϕ)\\sin θ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3\\cosh ϕ\\cos θ, 4\\sinh ϕ, \\cosh ϕ\\sin θ⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $[MATH: \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)]$ and $\\phi \\in \\mathbb R$.\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue} \\cosh{\\phi} \\sin\\theta}$."] [17592186175746 :question/problem "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi)$, given by\n$\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: 3\\sinh{\\phi} \\sin\\theta, \\: 3\\cosh{\\phi} \\big\\rangle$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x^2 + y^2 - z^2 = -9$. By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola $z^2-r^2 = k^2$ can be parametrized as $\\langle z,r\\rangle = \\langle k\\cosh\\phi, k\\sinh \\phi\\rangle$* for $\\phi\\in \\mathbb R$."] [17592186175746 :question/worked-solution "If $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad z = z\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + y^{2} - z^{2} &= -9 \\\\\n(r\\cos θ)^{2} + (r\\sin θ)^{2} - z^{2} &= -9 \\\\\nr^{2}(\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ) - z^{2} &= -9 \\\\\nr^{2} - z^{2} &= -9 \\\\\nz^{2} - r^{2} &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola\n\n$$\nz^2 - r^2 = 9\n$$\n\nrepresents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3597/Source/Images/875299fe0aa7ace760480a264f00ffdb.png)\n\nUsing the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n\\langle z, r \\rangle = \\langle 3\\cosh{\\phi}, \\: 3\\sinh{\\phi} \\rangle, \\qquad \\phi \\in \\mathbb R\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(θ, ϕ) &= ⟨x, y, z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ, z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨(3\\sinh ϕ)\\cos θ, (3\\sinh ϕ)\\sin θ, 3\\cosh ϕ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3\\sinh ϕ\\cos θ, 3\\sinh ϕ\\sin θ, 3\\cosh ϕ⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $[MATH: \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)]$ and $\\phi \\in \\mathbb R$.\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}3\\sinh{\\phi} \\cos\\theta}$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186173371 17592186173372 17592186173373 17592186173374 17592186173375 17592186173377 17592186173378 17592186173379 17592186173380 17592186173381 17592186173407 17592186173408 17592186173409 17592186173410 17592186173411 17592186174601 17592186174602 17592186174603 17592186174604 17592186174605] "immutable_fields" [[17592186173370 :answer-field/correct] [17592186173370 :answer-field/id] [17592186173370 :answer-field/key] [17592186173370 :answer-field/type] [17592186173376 :answer-field/correct] [17592186173376 :answer-field/id] [17592186173376 :answer-field/key] [17592186173376 :answer-field/type] [17592186173406 :answer-field/correct] [17592186173406 :answer-field/id] [17592186173406 :answer-field/key] [17592186173406 :answer-field/type] [17592186174600 :answer-field/correct] [17592186174600 :answer-field/id] [17592186174600 :answer-field/key] [17592186174600 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
